1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 ({"Many play here at once} 
5... Nxe4 {, but there is something to to be said for develop­ing instead,
Be7 being an admittedly excellent move about this point for Black."}) 6. d4
exd4 7. e5 Ne4 8. Nxd4 Nc5 9. Nf5 O-O ({"The lively variation which White now
enters upon renders the game interesting. Of course if} 9... Nxa4 {White gains
an advantage by} 10. Nxg7+ Kf8 {, followed by} 11. Bh6 {"}) 10. Qg4 g6 11. Bh6
({This is played occasionally, but players of the 2020s prefer the line} 11.
Bxc6 dxc6 12. Nxe7+ Qxe7 13. Qg3 Re8 14. Re1 Bf5 15. Bg5 f6 16. Bf4 {, which
is roughly equal. JS}) 11... Re8 12. Nc3 d5 13. Nxe7+ Qxe7 14. Qg3 (14. Qf3 $5)
14... Nxa4 {"This is the move that destroys all White’s chances," writes
Hoffer, but 21st century technology will prove him wrong.} 15. Nxd5 ({"} 15.
Nxa4 {apparently at once leads to a lost game."}) 15... Qxe5 16. Nxc7 ({
"This is rather better than} 16. Rae1 Qxe1 17. Nf6+ Kh8 18. Nxe8 ({But engines
demonstrate that White can win after} 18. Qh4 $3 {when, perhaps surprisingly,
Black can't find a way to meet White's threats such as 19 Bd2 or 19 Bf8, e.g.}
Qb4 (18... Qe5 19. Bf4 Kg7 20. Bxe5 Rxe5 21. f4 Re6 22. Qxh7+ $1 Kxf6 23. f5 $1
) 19. c4 Re5 (19... Qe7 20. Bf8 $1 h5 21. Bxe7 Rxe7 22. Qg5 {when Black's rook
and two minor pieces for the queen are no match for White's small commando
force.}) 20. Bd2 $1 Rh5 21. Nxh5 Qxb2 22. Nf6 $18) 18... Qxe8 {, etc. Both
courses lose."}) 16... Qxg3 17. fxg3 Be6 18. b3 Nc3 19. Nxa8 Rxa8 20. Bd2 Ne4 {
"and wins." Sources: Pall Mall Gazette, 12 January 1897 (which has a
crosstable); Illustrated London News, 23 January 1897} 0-1

